{templateType Trilobit\Core\Presentation\Styleguide\OverviewDefaultTemplate}

{*
 * The shell every page is drawn in, and its four regions.
 *
 * The specimen is a second shell drawn inside the first, with the same classes,
 * so that the switch above rearranges it the way it rearranges this page: its
 * areas, columns and rows are the theme's tokens and are left alone. Four other
 * tokens are set on the specimen, for it and nothing else, because a shell drawn
 * inside a page cannot behave the way the page does:
 *
 * - --layout-shell-min-size: auto. The page's shell is never shorter than the
 *   window, and a specimen that tall would be a page of its own. The navigation
 *   it holds is bounded by the same token, which then bounds nothing.
 * - --layout-banner-position and --layout-nav-position: static. A held band in
 *   the content would stick to the window under the page's own banner, and
 *   assets/chrome.ts adds up every band whose position is sticky - a held
 *   specimen would move where a jump to a heading stops.
 * - --layout-z-chrome: auto. The banner is a grid item, so its layer applies
 *   even where it is not held, and the specimen's banner would be drawn over
 *   the page's own as it scrolls under it.
 *
 * What is held, and the layer it is held in, is shown by the chrome of this
 * very page and explained on the page the first paragraph points at, which
 * stays where it is, under Foundations.
 *
 * The example content is invented and has to stay invented.
 *}

{import '../../templates/furniture.latte'}
{import '../../../components/nav.latte'}

{block sgPage}
    <p class="l-measure">
        Every page is written in the same four regions in the same order - banner, navigation,
        content, footer - and none of them knows where it will be drawn. The theme decides that:
        ledger puts the navigation in a column beside the other three, atrium puts all four one under
        another. Which of them stays in view while the page scrolls is a theme's decision too, and it is
        shown by this page's own banner and navigation: see
        <a n:href="page group: 'foundations', page: 'chrome'" data-testid="styleguide-chrome-link">what stays in view</a>.
    </p>

    {embed block sgLayoutSection,
        primitive: 'l-shell',
        summary: 'The grid a page is drawn in. Its areas, columns and rows are tokens of the theme, so moving '
            . 'the navigation from the top of the page to its side changes no template.'}
        {block sgLayoutSectionBody}
            {embed block sgSpecimen, variant: 'the four regions, placed by the theme'}
                {block sgSpecimenStage}
                    <div
                        class="l-shell"
                        style="--layout-shell-min-size: auto; --layout-banner-position: static; --layout-nav-position: static; --layout-z-chrome: auto"
                        data-testid="styleguide-shell-specimen"
                    >
                        <div class="l-shell__banner">
                            <p><code>.l-shell__banner</code> - the name of the site, and whoever is signed in</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="l-shell__nav">
                            <div class="l-shell__nav-hold">
                                {include nav, items: $sampleNavigation, label: 'Shell specimen'}
                            </div>
                        </div>

                        <div class="l-shell__main">
                            <p><code>.l-shell__main</code> - the content of the page</p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="l-shell__footer">
                            <p><code>.l-shell__footer</code> - what every page ends with</p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                {/block}
            {/embed}

            <p class="l-measure">
                The navigation region is <code>.l-shell__nav</code>, drawn here with the navigation
                component the way every page draws it. It paints the whole region, and what it holds is
                one element in, <code>.l-shell__nav-hold</code> - the part a theme keeps in view where the
                navigation is a column beside the page, because a region stretched down the whole page has
                nowhere to stick to. Where the navigation is a row under the banner, the region is no taller
                than what it holds, and it is the region that stays.
            </p>
        {/block}
    {/embed}
{/block}
